  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Miners broke into a cave of crystals bigger than trees. April 2000, three hundred metres under Chihuahua, in northern Mexico.",
        "visual_query": "mexican desert mine chihuahua landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "desert mine"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They only got there because the pumps were running. Naica is a working silver and lead mine, kept dry by force.",
        "visual_query": "underground mine tunnel headlamps",
        "visual_fallback": "mine tunnel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Inside lay beams of stone. The largest is 11.4 metres long and weighs roughly twelve tonnes. So why did they stop growing?",
        "visual_query": "giant translucent gypsum crystal cave",
        "visual_fallback": "crystal cave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They are selenite, and they need water held for ages at almost exactly the temperature gypsum keeps crystallising at.",
        "visual_query": "mineral rich groundwater cave chamber",
        "visual_fallback": "cave water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That water was the pumps' problem. Fifty-eight degrees, near total humidity, ten minutes a person. In 2015 the pumps stopped and it drowned again.",
        "visual_query": "flooded mine shaft rising water",
        "visual_fallback": "flooded shaft"
      }
    ],
